1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,578
Hello, this is Liam, in for Bear.

2
00:00:01,628 --> 00:00:05,377
What if your textbook could actually
answer your questions and show its work?

3
00:00:05,427 --> 00:00:09,473
Most digital textbooks are PDFs with
better fonts: you read, you guess, you
hope it

4
00:00:09,523 --> 00:00:12,384
sticks. Medhavy grounds an AI tutor in the
textbook itself.

5
00:00:12,434 --> 00:00:16,380
It knows the book, the chapter you are on,
and you, so the answer is one question

6
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:18,551
away and you always know where it came
from.

7
00:00:18,601 --> 00:00:21,611
Suggested prompt, real screens, my own
account, names masked.

8
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:24,889
Here is the correction. This is not a
chatbot bolted onto a PDF.

9
00:00:24,939 --> 00:00:29,227
Medhavy knows which book you are in, which
chapter you are on, what you have already

10
00:00:29,277 --> 00:00:31,499
asked, and which course handed you the
book.

11
00:00:31,549 --> 00:00:35,269
Every answer is pulled from that context,
and it lists the pages it used.

12
00:00:35,319 --> 00:00:37,333
Let us walk in the way a student would.

13
00:00:37,333 --> 00:00:42,368
Start at hub dot medhavy dot com. This is
the front door to every textbook.

14
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:46,127
Sign in with your email or username and
your password.

15
00:00:46,177 --> 00:00:50,449
I am not typing mine on camera, so the
next cut is me already inside.

16
00:00:52,333 --> 00:00:52,790
New here?

17
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:57,347
Create an account, then ask your
instructor or an admin for access to your
book.

18
00:00:58,533 --> 00:01:03,346
Signed in, you get a dashboard: books you
can open, books pending, books to request.

19
00:01:03,396 --> 00:01:07,621
Mine is the admin shelf, every book. Click
the blue Open Textbook button.

20
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:11,911
The book opens in its own tab.

21
00:01:11,961 --> 00:01:16,690
Two ways to learn, it says: ask the AI
tutor, or browse the textbook. Both,
really.

22
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:20,943
Getting started, the beta disclaimer,
attribution: all on the front page.

23
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:27,845
The left sidebar lists every chapter and
section, so you jump straight to a topic.

24
00:01:27,895 --> 00:01:29,903
Chapter five, oncogenes, section two.

25
00:01:29,953 --> 00:01:34,353
Each section reads like a modern online
book: clear headings, figures with their

26
00:01:34,403 --> 00:01:38,352
source, key terms underlined, and an on-
this-page outline on the right.

27
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:45,977
Command K searches the whole book, keyword
and meaning together.

28
00:01:46,027 --> 00:01:48,932
Warburg effect: chapter fourteen, chapter
thirty.

29
00:01:49,833 --> 00:01:52,890
The key feature is the panel on the right:
ask this textbook.

30
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:55,589
Suggested prompts for this section, or
type your own.

31
00:01:55,639 --> 00:01:58,848
What is the difference between an oncogene
and a proto-oncogene?

32
00:01:58,898 --> 00:02:03,126
The answer streams in using this chapter
as context, and it lists the top sources
it

33
00:02:03,176 --> 00:02:05,620
used first, so you can check where it came
from.

34
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:11,943
Follow up without re-explaining.

35
00:02:11,993 --> 00:02:16,823
Give me a worked example with my own
numbers: two copies of MYC amplified to
twenty.

36
00:02:16,873 --> 00:02:21,587
It remembers the thread within the
chapter, uses your numbers, and calls it a
ten-

37
00:02:21,637 --> 00:02:22,741
fold amplification.

38
00:02:27,533 --> 00:02:29,662
When you need it simpler, say so.

39
00:02:29,712 --> 00:02:34,612
Explain retroviral transduction in simpler
terms with a real-world analogy.

40
00:02:34,662 --> 00:02:40,141
Step-by-step derivations, variable
definitions, analogies for equations: same
move.

41
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:48,150
Quick recap: give me a summary of this
section in three lines.

42
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:52,890
And the panel's own footnote stays put: AI
can make mistakes, verify important

43
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:54,033
information. Good.

44
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:00,438
Night mode for long reads.

45
00:03:00,488 --> 00:03:04,891
Reliable text on the page, an assistant
beside it that cites the book.

46
00:03:04,941 --> 00:03:06,023
You set the pace.

47
00:03:09,233 --> 00:03:13,163
What I saw work: sign-in through the hub,
one tab per book, chapters with figures

48
00:03:13,213 --> 00:03:16,946
and sources, search, and a tutor that
answers from the chapter, shows its top

49
00:03:16,996 --> 00:03:18,616
sources, and remembers the thread.

50
00:03:18,666 --> 00:03:22,743
What to watch: in one answer the listed
sources wandered into angiogenesis
sections,

51
00:03:22,793 --> 00:03:24,757
so read the sources, not just the answer.

52
00:03:24,807 --> 00:03:28,785
Not shown: the student dashboard counts,
because I am on an admin account, and the

53
00:03:28,835 --> 00:03:29,671
instructor tools.

54
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:30,187
Your turn.

55
00:03:30,237 --> 00:03:34,377
Open your book from the hub, go to the
section you are stuck on, and ask it to

56
00:03:34,427 --> 00:03:38,298
explain in simple terms, then for a worked
example with your own numbers.

57
00:03:38,348 --> 00:03:42,273
Read the sources it lists before you trust
it. One clear answer at a time.

58
00:03:42,323 --> 00:03:43,289
Liam, in for Bear.

59
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:48,868
Cancer Biology and Therapeutics: A
Textbook That Answers Back. At Nik Bear
Brown.
